11.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The question requires identification of the single statement about Augustine on free will that cannot be defended as accurate. Augustine’s own texts (especially De libero arbitrio, the anti-Pelagian treatises, and De gratia et libero arbitrio) plus the standard scholarly literature allow A, B, D, E, and F to stand, each reflecting either a documented Augustinian position or a recognized line of later commentary.

C fails on two independent grounds. First, the phrase bonae voluntatis is taken directly from the Vulgate rendering of Luke 2:14; Augustine repeatedly cites the biblical wording, not a Senecan source. Second, while Seneca (and other Stoics) supplied part of the late-antique vocabulary of volition, Augustine does not present voluntas as merely one faculty “within the psyche” in the manner of Seneca’s moral psychology; he treats it as the whole orientation of the rational soul toward or away from God. The claim that Augustine “echoing Seneca, determined that the voluntas was a part of the human psyche” therefore misattributes both the textual origin and the conceptual framing of the idea. Hence C is the statement that is not correct.}

